# TODO - Title onboarding

## Objective

Establish the shared title package and onboarding convention, demonstrated
by the first catalogue title. Subsequent titles use the same convention
in catalogue order, after the foundation and build gates.

## Acceptance

Shared foundation milestone:

- Use the existing catalogue order; no preliminary ranking audit needed.
- Demonstrate the convention on the first title, with its supplied source
  edition, canonical task record, workspace package and inline tests.
- Use `crates/games/<id>` or `crates/apps/<id>` and inline behavioural tests.
- Reference the canonical task record rather than duplicating its contents.
- Demonstrate reuse of shared mechanisms and the shared build selection
  and portable library validation on the first title.

Per-title acceptance remains mandatory under AGENTS.md and every title's
own record. It is evaluated before that title closes, not as a prerequisite
for the shared P2 onboarding milestone:

- Pin one preferred source edition per title when supplied.
- Keep only unique gameplay and adapters in each title package.
- Before implementing each new title, review every previously implemented
  game and app package, including unfinished titles, without exception.
  Inspect their code and tests as well as existing shared capabilities.
- Extract genuinely reusable mechanisms into their owning shared crates
  before duplicating them. Migrate existing consumers and retain their
  behavioural regression tests. Keep only unique rules and data in titles.
- Record the reviewed title identifiers, reused or extracted mechanisms,
  and justified title-specific exceptions in the existing task evidence.
  Recheck newly introduced mechanisms for reuse before closing the task.
- Implement adaptive height-fit world rendering for each title, manually
  adapting camera, scene data and HUD where necessary. Retain the original
  vertical field and uniform scale, render real additional scene content
  at wider aspect ratios, and adapt input transforms without changing
  simulation behaviour. Preserve original presentation as an option.
  Treat unsupported scenes as open work, with temporary aspect-fit fallback
  and documented limitations; do not count fallback as adaptive support.

## Evidence

The first catalogue title has a dedicated Rust package at
`crates/games/simpsons-arcade`, with the same identifier as its canonical
record and inline deterministic behavioural tests. Its verified source
edition and local layout are recorded in that title task. No earlier
implemented title package existed when it was introduced. The package
owns its gameplay and runtime composition; shared engine timing, logical
input, replay, menus, presentation, storage, audio and network mechanisms
remain in their respective shared owners.

The build selector admits the exact title identifier and explicit native
feature/target combination. Its synthetic regression rejects unknown
titles, path-like identifiers, unsupported targets and extra arguments.
The validator classifies game and app packages automatically for portable
library checks and runtime dependency isolation. It rejects binary-only
title packages, misplaced workspace files and noncanonical source layout.

The first title's original-backed native preview has demonstrated real
height-fit street extension, not stretched or duplicated edge pixels.
At 1b64256 the existing 218-pose diagnostic verified the 444-column
world camera, 600 movement and 20 combat submissions in the admitted
presentation modes, including adaptive with touch. The later visible
settings reset work passed native-enabled workspace validation
1795662082554524617-58037 (1,719 aggregate test executions and six
portable core targets). Other title scenes, full gameplay parity,
physical devices and future title onboarding remain open in their
respective canonical title and platform records.

The shared milestone is independent of the remaining catalogue packages:
requiring all of their implementation and adaptive scenes before the
build command would prevent the sequential P4 title work from starting.
The per-title acceptance obligations above are unchanged and may not be
claimed as passed merely because this shared convention is demonstrated.

Commit 1d7f686 makes title-task linkage mandatory in the validator:
package directory identifiers and areas must match their canonical task,
completed title tasks must have a package, and untracked orphan packages
are rejected. Future open title records may remain unimplemented. The
fresh validation run 1795665047137724133-520482 passed.
